<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Tummel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Tummel is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It flows east from Loch Rannoch through Loch Tummel and past Pitlochry, where a hydroelectric dam forms Loch Faskally, and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tay">River Tay</a> near Ballinluig. Together with the Garry it drains much of the historic district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atholl">Atholl</a>, whose valleys formed the core of an early Pictish kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-tummel.jpg" alt="River Tummel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Val Vannet · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Tummel_near_Pitlochry_-_geograph.org.uk_-_40694.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
